  <h2 id="build"><span class="num">2.1</span> Build generative applications by using Foundry</h2>

  <h3 id="connect">Connecting an application to a Foundry project</h3>
  <p>
    This is an explicit exam objective, and the answer is short: one endpoint, one credential, one client.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`from azure.identity import DefaultAzureCredential
from azure.ai.projects import AIProjectClient

project = AIProjectClient(
    endpoint="https://<resource>.services.ai.azure.com/api/projects/<project>",
    credential=DefaultAzureCredential(),
)
openai = project.get_openai_client()          # plain model calls
agent_client = project.get_openai_client(agent_name="policy-assistant")  # bound to an agent`}
    lang="python"
    label="Python · project client"
  />

  <Callout type="code-note" title="Things that must be exactly right">
    <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
      <li>The endpoint form is <code>https://&lt;resource&gt;.services.ai.azure.com/api/projects/&lt;project&gt;</code> — the <code>/api/projects/</code> segment is not optional.</li>
      <li>Package is <strong><code>azure-ai-projects</code> 2.x</strong> (<code>pip install "azure-ai-projects&gt;=2.3.0" azure-identity</code>). 1.x is classic and incompatible.</li>
      <li><code>DefaultAzureCredential</code> needs <code>az login</code> locally and a managed identity in Azure — plus a <strong>Foundry User</strong> role assignment. Missing the role is the usual cause of a 401/403 in a question.</li>
      <li>For REST, the token scope is <code>https://ai.azure.com/.default</code> and routes live under <code>/openai/v1/</code>.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h3 id="consume-models">Deploying and consuming models</h3>
  <p>
    Once a model is deployed, its <strong>deployment name</strong> is what you pass as <code>model=</code>. The same
    call shape works across LLMs, small models, code models and multimodal models — which is exactly why the exam can
    ask you to swap models without changing code.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`# Text
r = openai.responses.create(model="gpt-5-mini", input="Explain RAG in two sentences.")

# Multimodal: text + image in one input item
r = openai.responses.create(
    model="gpt-5",
    input=[{
        "role": "user",
        "content": [
            {"type": "input_text", "text": "What safety issues can you see on this site?"},
            {"type": "input_image", "image_url": "https://example.com/site.jpg"},
        ],
    }],
)

# Streaming, for perceived latency
with openai.responses.stream(model="gpt-5-mini", input=prompt) as stream:
    for event in stream:
        if event.type == "response.output_text.delta":
            print(event.delta, end="")`}
    lang="python"
    label="Python · consuming models"
  />

  <h3 id="rag">Implementing retrieval-augmented generation</h3>

  <Mermaid code={ragFlow} caption="RAG has two halves that fail independently: an offline ingestion pipeline and an online retrieval-and-generation path." />

  <p>Three ways to do RAG in Foundry, in increasing order of control:</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl t-magenta">
      <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>File search tool</strong></td>
          <td>Upload files to the project, create a vector store, attach the <code>file_search</code> tool</td>
          <td>Fastest path; Foundry owns chunking, embedding and the vector store. Reuse a <strong>persistent</strong> vector store in production rather than creating one per run</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Azure AI Search tool</strong></td>
          <td>Connect an existing index and attach the <code>azure_ai_search</code> tool; answers come back with inline citations</td>
          <td>Full control of the index, filters, hybrid search and relevance. The index needs searchable/retrievable <code>Edm.String</code> fields and searchable <code>Collection(Edm.Single)</code> vector fields</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Foundry IQ knowledge base</strong></td>
          <td>Define knowledge sources, group them into a knowledge base, point agents at it</td>
          <td>Managed, permission-aware, reusable across agents; adds agentic retrieval with query planning</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <CodeBlock code={ragAgent} lang="python" label="Python · RAG agent" />

  <Callout type="exam" title="The instructions are part of the RAG design">
    <p>
      Notice what the system instructions above do: restrict the answer to retrieved content, require citations, and
      define the behaviour when the documents do not cover the question. Exam items about "the model invents policies
      that do not exist" want <strong>grounding plus an explicit refusal instruction</strong>, measured with the
      <strong>groundedness</strong> evaluator — not a lower temperature on its own.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h3 id="workflows">Workflows, tool-augmented flows and multi-step reasoning</h3>
  <p>
    Foundry <strong>workflows</strong> <span class="pill p-amber">preview</span> orchestrate multiple agents in a
    repeatable process, with branching, variables and human steps, authored in the portal or as YAML in VS Code. Three
    starting templates map directly to the orchestration patterns:
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl t-magenta">
      <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Behaviour</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Human in the loop</strong></td><td>Asks the user a question and waits for input before continuing</td><td>Approval requests, clarifying questions</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sequential</strong></td><td>Passes each agent's result to the next in a fixed order</td><td>Pipelines, staged processing, auditable approval chains</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Group chat</strong></td><td>Passes control between agents dynamically based on context or rules</td><td>Escalation, fallback, expert handoff</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    For deterministic, long-running or event-driven business processes, <strong>Azure Logic Apps</strong> is the
    complementary answer: Foundry hosts and grounds the agent, Logic Apps triggers it from events or schedules, waits
    on long-running approvals and exposes connectors as tools.
  </p>

  <h3 id="evaluate">Evaluating models and applications</h3>
  <p>
    The outline calls out four things to evaluate for: <strong>fabrications, relevance, quality and safety</strong>.
    Those map onto evaluator families directly.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl t-magenta">
      <thead><tr><th>Concern in the question</th><th>Evaluator</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>"The answer states facts not in the source documents"</td><td><strong>Groundedness</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"The answer is true but does not address the question"</td><td><strong>Relevance</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"We retrieved the wrong documents"</td><td><strong>Retrieval</strong> / document retrieval</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"The writing is rambling or incoherent"</td><td><strong>Coherence</strong>, <strong>fluency</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Could it produce harmful content?"</td><td>Safety evaluators, plus the <strong>AI red teaming agent</strong> (PyRIT)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Did the agent call the right tool with the right arguments?"</td><td><strong>Tool call accuracy</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Did the agent understand what was asked?"</td><td><strong>Intent resolution</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Did the agent finish the job and stay on task?"</td><td><strong>Task completion</strong>, <strong>task adherence</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"It must satisfy our own domain rule"</td><td><strong>Custom evaluator</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <CodeBlock code={evalCode} lang="python" label="Python · evaluation" />

  <Callout type="tip" title="Evaluation vocabulary that shows up in distractors">
    <p>
      <strong>Benchmarks</strong> compare candidate models on public or your own data — that is <em>model
      selection</em>. <strong>Evaluations</strong> score your application's outputs against a dataset — that is
      <em>pre-production</em>. <strong>Continuous evaluation</strong> samples live traffic and
      <strong>scheduled evaluation</strong> re-runs a fixed dataset to detect drift — that is <em>post-production</em>.
      <strong>Cluster analysis</strong> groups failures so you can see the pattern.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <h2 id="agents"><span class="num">2.2</span> Build agents by using Foundry</h2>

  <h3 id="agent-anatomy">Roles, goals, conversation tracking and tool schemas</h3>
  <p>An agent definition is four decisions, and the exam tests each separately.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl t-magenta">
      <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>What good looks like</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Role and goal</strong></td>
          <td><code>instructions</code></td>
          <td>Persona, scope, tone, the boundaries of what it will answer, what to do when it cannot, and how to cite</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Model</strong></td>
          <td><code>model</code> — the deployment name</td>
          <td>Swappable without changing the rest of the definition</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Conversation tracking</strong></td>
          <td>A <strong>conversation</strong> id per user session; or <code>previous_response_id</code> for a simple chain; or your own store</td>
          <td>Server-side conversations are the default. Bring your own Cosmos DB when you must own the data</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Tool schemas</strong></td>
          <td><code>tools</code> — JSON Schema per function, or built-in tool configs</td>
          <td>Precise names, descriptions that say <em>when</em> to use and <em>when not to</em>, enums over free strings, <code>required</code> set honestly</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <Mermaid code={agentLoop} caption="One agent turn. The guardrail runs at up to four points, and everything the agent does is persisted as items on the conversation." />

  <h3 id="function-calling">Function calling</h3>
  <p>
    Function calling is client-side: the model decides <em>that</em> a function should be called and with which
    arguments; <strong>your code executes it</strong> and returns the result. That is why function calling cannot live
    inside a toolbox — the execution happens on your side.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock code={functionTool} lang="python" label="Python · function tool" />

  <Callout type="exam" title="Tool descriptions are prompt engineering">
    <p>
      "The agent calls the wrong tool" or "the agent calls the API when it should have answered from documentation" is
      almost never fixed by changing the model. The fix is <strong>better tool names and descriptions</strong>,
      <strong>enums instead of free-text parameters</strong>, and instructions that state the selection rule. At scale
      (dozens of tools), the answer is <strong>tool search</strong> in a toolbox, which hides tools behind
      <code>tool_search</code> and <code>call_tool</code> meta-tools so definitions stop consuming the context window.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h3 id="structured">Structured outputs</h3>
  <p>
    When downstream code consumes the output, constrain generation to a schema rather than parsing prose. This is also
    the answer to "produce structured JSON output" in Skill 4.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock code={structuredOutput} lang="python" label="Python · structured output" />

  <h3 id="tools">Tools and toolboxes</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>toolbox</strong> curates tools once and exposes them through a single managed
    <strong>MCP-compatible endpoint</strong>, with central authentication, guardrails, observability and
    <strong>versioning</strong>. Agents point at the toolbox, so you can add, remove or reconfigure tools — or promote a
    new default version — <strong>without changing or redeploying agent code</strong>. Toolboxes are Foundry-homed but
    not Foundry-bound: any MCP-compatible runtime (Agent Framework, LangGraph, your own code) can consume one.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl t-magenta">
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Does</th><th>In a toolbox?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>MCP</strong></td><td>Connect a remote Model Context Protocol server</td><td>✔</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Web search</strong></td><td>Public web results. Start here before Grounding with Bing</td><td>✔</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Azure AI Search</strong></td><td>Retrieve from your index with inline citations</td><td>✔</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>File search</strong></td><td>Search project files and vector stores</td><td>✔</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Code interpreter</strong></td><td>Run code in a sandbox for data work and charts</td><td>✔</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>OpenAPI</strong></td><td>Call any REST API from its spec</td><td>✔</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Agent-to-agent (A2A)</strong></td><td>Call another agent over the A2A protocol</td><td>✔</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Browser automation</strong></td><td>Drive a real browser</td><td>✔</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Fabric IQ / Work IQ</strong></td><td>Structured data in Fabric; Microsoft 365 work context</td><td>✔</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Tool search</strong> <span class="pill p-amber">preview</span></td><td>Surface only relevant tools at runtime</td><td>✔ (toolbox only)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Skills</strong> <span class="pill p-amber">preview</span></td><td>Versioned, immutable multi-step workflows the agent invokes like a tool</td><td>✔ (toolbox only)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Function calling</strong></td><td>Your own code, executed client-side</td><td><strong>✘</strong> — client-side execution</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Grounding with Bing</strong> / Bing Custom Search</td><td>Real-time public web with source attribution</td><td><strong>✘</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Computer use</strong>, <strong>image generation</strong>, <strong>SharePoint</strong>, <strong>Fabric data agent</strong>, <strong>Azure Functions</strong></td><td>Direct integration only</td><td><strong>✘</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <Callout type="warn" title="Grounding with Bing has compliance strings attached">
    <p>
      Grounding with Bing Search and Bing Custom Search are <strong>First Party Consumption Services</strong> governed by
      the Grounding with Bing terms, <strong>not</strong> the Microsoft Data Protection Addendum. Data sent to them
      <strong>flows outside the Azure compliance and geo boundary</strong>, and using them <strong>waives elevated
      Government Community Cloud commitments</strong> including data sovereignty. They also cost extra. If a scenario
      stresses data residency or sovereignty, Bing grounding is the wrong answer — and
      <strong>Bing Custom Search</strong> is the one that restricts grounding to a chosen subset of the web.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p><strong>Tools vs skills</strong> is a distinction the outline implies: tools define <em>what</em> an agent can do; skills define <em>how</em> it performs a multi-step task. Skills are versioned and immutable, and agents discover them automatically through MCP resources at startup.</p>

  <h3 id="memory">Conversation memory</h3>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`# Server-side conversation: history is maintained for you.
conversation = openai.conversations.create()

r1 = openai.responses.create(conversation=conversation.id, input="Book me a room in Lisbon.")
r2 = openai.responses.create(conversation=conversation.id, input="Make it two nights.")
# r2 knows what "it" refers to.

# Lightweight alternative: chain by response id, no conversation object.
r1 = openai.responses.create(model="gpt-5-mini", input="Summarise this contract.")
r2 = openai.responses.create(model="gpt-5-mini", previous_response_id=r1.id, input="Now list the risks.")`}
    lang="python"
    label="Python · conversation state"
  />

  <p>
    <strong>Semantic memory</strong> is the layer above: a memory provider extracts durable facts and summaries from
    conversations, stores them by <strong>scope</strong> (typically a stable user or tenant id your application owns)
    and retrieves the relevant ones in later sessions. Extraction is <strong>asynchronous</strong> — if the next step
    depends on a newly written memory, you must wait for it. Keep exact transcripts in a history provider if you need
    complete records; memory deliberately stores facts, not the conversation.
  </p>

  <h3 id="orchestration">Multi-agent orchestration</h3>

  <Mermaid code={multiAgent} caption="Four patterns. The one the exam most often distinguishes is handoff (ownership transfers) versus agent-as-tool (ownership is retained)." />

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl t-magenta">
      <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Control flow</th><th>Use it when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Sequential</strong></td><td>Fixed order, each output feeds the next</td><td>Deterministic pipelines, staged approvals, auditability</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Concurrent</strong></td><td>Fan out in parallel, then aggregate</td><td>Independent perspectives on the same input; latency matters</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Handoff</strong></td><td>An agent <strong>transfers ownership</strong> of the whole task; the receiver has full context; no central authority</td><td>Triage and routing to specialists, dynamic escalation</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Group chat</strong></td><td>Agents collaborate in a shared conversation, control passed by context or rules</td><td>Debate, review, fallback</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Magentic</strong></td><td>A manager agent dynamically coordinates specialists</td><td>Open-ended problems with no fixed plan</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Agent-as-tool</strong></td><td>A primary agent <strong>delegates a subtask</strong> and <strong>keeps ownership</strong>; the specialist returns a result</td><td>A specialist capability inside a larger task the primary agent still owns</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <Callout type="exam" title="Handoff vs agent-as-tool, stated plainly">
    <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
      <li><strong>Control flow:</strong> handoff passes control permanently; agent-as-tool returns control to the caller.</li>
      <li><strong>Task ownership:</strong> the receiving agent owns the task after a handoff; the primary agent retains responsibility with agent-as-tool.</li>
      <li><strong>Context:</strong> handoff gives the receiver the full conversation; agent-as-tool passes only what the primary chooses to share.</li>
      <li>Scenario cue: "route the customer to the refunds specialist who then handles the case" → <strong>handoff</strong>. "Ask the translation agent to translate this paragraph, then carry on" → <strong>agent-as-tool</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h3 id="safeguards">Autonomy, safeguards and approval controls</h3>
  <p>
    "Build autonomous or semi-autonomous workflows with safeguards and approval flow controls" is an objective in its
    own right. The mechanisms:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Approval-required tools</strong> — mark a tool so the workflow pauses and emits an approval request before it executes. This is the standard answer for refunds, purchases, deletions and anything irreversible.</li>
    <li><strong>Autonomous mode with turn limits</strong> — let an agent proceed without the user for a bounded number of turns, so it cannot loop indefinitely.</li>
    <li><strong>Human-in-the-loop request/response</strong> — a request port that pauses the workflow for free-form human input, not just approve/reject.</li>
    <li><strong>Checkpointing</strong> — persist workflow state so an approval that arrives hours or days later can resume the run. Essential for long-running processes.</li>
    <li><strong>Guardrails on tool call and tool response</strong> — scan what the agent sends to tools and what comes back.</li>
    <li><strong>Scoped agent identity and egress controls</strong> — limit what the agent can reach at all.</li>
  </ul>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`from typing import Annotated
from agent_framework import tool

# The workflow pauses and emits an approval request before this ever runs.
@tool(approval_mode="always_require")
def process_refund(order_number: Annotated[str, "Order number to refund"]) -> str:
    """Process a refund for the given order."""
    return f"Refund processed for {order_number}."`}
    lang="python"
    label="Python · approval gate"
  />

  <h3 id="agent-monitoring">Monitoring agents and error analysis</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tracing</strong> shows every model call, tool invocation and decision in one timeline — the only practical way to debug "why did it do that?".</li>
    <li><strong>Agent evaluators</strong> — intent resolution, tool call accuracy, task adherence, task completion — turn behaviour into numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>Monitoring dashboards</strong> track latency, token consumption, error rates and quality scores in production.</li>
    <li><strong>Cluster analysis</strong> groups failing evaluation cases so you fix a class of problem rather than one row.</li>
    <li><strong>Agent optimizer</strong> <span class="pill p-amber">preview</span> evaluates behaviour and automatically generates better instructions, skills, tool descriptions and model selections.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="tip" title="Error analysis, in the order that works">
    <p>
      Open the trace. Did it <strong>misunderstand</strong> (intent resolution low) → fix instructions. Did it pick the
      <strong>wrong tool</strong> (tool call accuracy low) → fix tool names, descriptions and schemas. Did the
      <strong>tool fail or return junk</strong> → fix the integration, not the prompt. Did it have the right data and
      still answer badly (groundedness low) → fix the prompt or the model. Did it <strong>stop early</strong> (task
      completion low) → check turn limits, token limits and instructions.
    </p>
  </Callout>

