  <h2 id="optimize"><span class="num">2.3</span> Optimise and operationalise</h2>

  <h3 id="prompting">Prompt engineering</h3>
  <p>Techniques the exam names, and what each is actually for:</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl t-magenta">
      <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>What it does</th><th>Reach for it when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Clear system instructions</strong></td><td>Sets persona, scope, tone, refusal behaviour and output contract</td><td>Always — it is the highest-leverage change available</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Few-shot examples</strong></td><td>Demonstrates the format and edge cases</td><td>Output shape or style is hard to describe but easy to show</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Chain of thought</strong></td><td>Asks for reasoning steps before the answer</td><td>Multi-step reasoning. Note: reasoning models do this internally — prompting them to "think step by step" adds little</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Grounding / context injection</strong></td><td>Puts retrieved facts in the prompt</td><td>Anything factual about private or recent data</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Structured outputs</strong></td><td>Constrains generation to a JSON Schema</td><td>Machine-consumed output — more reliable than "respond in JSON"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Delimiters and spotlighting</strong></td><td>Marks untrusted content as data rather than instructions</td><td>Any prompt containing retrieved or user-supplied content — the XPIA defence</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Prompt caching</strong></td><td>Reuses a stable prefix across calls</td><td>Long, unchanging system prompts at volume — a cost and latency lever</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="parameters">Model parameters</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl t-magenta">
      <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Effect</th><th>Guidance</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>temperature</code></td><td>Randomness of sampling. 0 is near-deterministic; higher is more varied</td><td>Low for extraction, classification and RAG; higher for creative copy</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>top_p</code></td><td>Nucleus sampling — restricts to the top probability mass</td><td><strong>Tune one of temperature or top_p, not both</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>max_output_tokens</code></td><td>Caps the response length</td><td>Also feeds the <strong>TPM rate-limit estimate</strong>, so an inflated value causes needless 429s</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>frequency_penalty</code></td><td>Discourages repeating the same tokens</td><td>Fixes visible repetition</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>presence_penalty</code></td><td>Encourages introducing new topics</td><td>Fixes a model that keeps circling one idea</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>stop</code></td><td>Sequences that end generation</td><td>Structured or templated output</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>seed</code></td><td>Best-effort reproducibility</td><td>Testing and evaluation runs</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>reasoning effort</code></td><td>How long a reasoning model thinks</td><td>Trades latency and cost for accuracy</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <Callout type="exam" title="Determinism questions have a standard answer">
    <p>
      "Responses vary between identical runs; we need consistency for a compliance workflow" →
      <strong>temperature 0</strong> (or very low), optionally a <code>seed</code>, plus
      <strong>structured outputs</strong> to lock the shape. Temperature 0 is not a guarantee of identical text —
      "best effort", not "reproducible" — so if the question demands a stable <em>schema</em>, structured outputs is the
      stronger answer.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h3 id="reflection">Reflection, chain-of-thought evaluation and self-critique</h3>
  <p>
    An explicit objective. These are patterns you implement, not switches you flip:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Self-critique</strong> — the model produces an answer, then a second pass criticises it against explicit criteria and revises. Costs an extra call or two; measurably improves quality on hard tasks.</li>
    <li><strong>Reflection loop</strong> — generate → evaluate → revise, repeated until a quality bar is met or an iteration cap is hit. <strong>Always set the cap.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Chain-of-thought evaluation</strong> — score the reasoning trace, not just the final answer, to find where a multi-step task went wrong.</li>
    <li><strong>LLM-as-judge</strong> — a separate model (the judge, configured in the evaluator) scores outputs. This is how the built-in AI-assisted evaluators work.</li>
    <li><strong>Agent optimizer</strong> <span class="pill p-amber">preview</span> — the managed version: it evaluates behaviour and rewrites instructions and tool descriptions for you.</li>
  </ul>

